INT. 24-HOUR LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

Fluorescent hum. Dryers turning, nobody watching.

WREN, thirties, sits on a folding table with a paperback open. She hasn't turned a page in ten minutes. Across the room, TEO, nineteen, feeds quarters into a machine that doesn't need them.

A STRANGER in a wet raincoat enters. Sets a duffel on the counter. Looks at neither of them.

STRANGER
Machine four broken?

WREN
Eats quarters.

He nods. Doesn't move. She turns a page. Teo drops a coin. It rolls. Nobody picks it up.

MRS. ABARA, seventies, folds towels behind the counter, slow and exact.

MRS. ABARA
Eleven-forty. Dryers lock at midnight.

STRANGER
Plenty of time.

He unzips the duffel one inch. Zips it again. Wren watches the zipper, then the book.

WREN
You're Baz.

BAZ
Who's asking.

WREN
Nobody. Machine four's yours.

He takes the duffel to machine four. Opens the door. Climbs the stool. Reaches behind the drum.

Teo, still not looking, feeds in another quarter.
